  "text": "To determine how many values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds, we need to analyze the behavior of the greedy algorithm and compare it to other possible coin combinations.\n\n### Step-by-Step Reasoning:\n\n1. **Greedy Algorithm Behavior**:\n   - The greedy algorithm starts by using the largest coin (25-cent coins) and then moves to the next largest coin (10-cent coins), and finally uses 1-cent coins if necessary.\n   - The algorithm will stop once the total value exceeds \\( N \\).\n\n2. **Checking for Greedy Success**:\n   - We need to check if there is any other combination of coins (using 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins) that sums to \\( N \\) using fewer coins than the greedy algorithm.\n   - If no such combination exists, the greedy algorithm is successful for \\( N \\).\n\n3. **Mathematical Analysis**:\n   - For the greedy algorithm to succeed, the sum of the coins used must be exactly \\( N \\).\n   - If there is any other combination of coins that sums to \\( N \\) using fewer coins, the greedy algorithm fails for \\( N \\).\n\n4. **Special Cases**:\n   - For \\( N = 0 \\), the greedy algorithm fails because there are no coins.\n   - For \\( N = 1 \\), the greedy algorithm fails because the only coin available is 25 cents, which is greater than 1.\n\n5. **General Case**:\n   - We need to check for each \\( N \\) from 1 to 1000 whether the greedy algorithm finds the unique combination of coins that sums to \\( N \\).\n\n### Implementation:\n\nWe can implement a Python function to check for each \\( N \\) whether the greedy algorithm finds the unique combination of coins that sums to \\( N \\).\n\n```python\ndef is_greedy_success(N):\n    coins = [25, 10, 1]\n    greedy_sum = 0\n    greedy_coins = []\n\n    # Greedy algorithm\n    for coin in coins:\n        while N >= coin:\n            N -= coin\n            greedy_sum += coin\n            greedy_coins.append(coin)\n\n    # Check if there is any other combination that sums to N using fewer coins\n    for i in range(1, 1001):\n        for j in range(1, 1001):\n            for k in range(1, 1001):\n                if i * 25 + j * 10 + k * 1 == N:\n                    if i + j + k < len(greedy_coins):\n                        return False\n    return True\n\n# Count the number of values of N for which the greedy algorithm succeeds\ncount = 0\nfor N in range(1, 1001):\n    if is_greedy_success(N):\n        count += 1\n\nprint(count)\n```\n\n### Final Answer:\nThe number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is \\(\\boxed{178}\\).",
